# A DDR3 SPD image laid out so that a short chunk read is *observable*.
#
# `ipmi_rawspd_main()' copies `maxread' bytes out of every response no matter
# how many the BMC actually returned, so a short response leaves the tail of
# the *previous* one in place.  With the default 64-byte chunk, byte k of the
# response for offset 0 lands at spd_data[k] and, when the read at offset 64 is
# short, byte k of that same stale buffer lands again at spd_data[64+k].
#
# So the manufacturer/date/serial block lives here at [53:61] rather than at
# [117:125], and reaches [117:125] only through that second, stale copy.  A
# port that copied `rsp->data_len' bytes instead would leave [117:125] zero and
# print a different manufacturer, date and serial number.  Used by
# spd_short_chunk_keeps_stale with tests/transcripts/spd_hole.tr.

92           # [  0] bytes used 176, SPD device size 256
11           # [  1] SPD revision 1.1
0b           # [  2] memory type 0Bh = DDR3 SDRAM
02           # [  3] module type 02h = UDIMM
13           # [  4] 8 banks, 4 Gbit density -> SDRAM capacity 2048 MB
11           # [  5] row/column addressing
02           # [  6] module nominal voltage: 1.5 V yes, 1.35 V no
0b           # [  7] 2 ranks, SDRAM device width 8 bits
0b           # [  8] primary bus width 64 bits, 8 bits of ECC
11           # [  9]
01           # [ 10]
08           # [ 11]
0a           # [ 12]
@pad 40 0x00   # [ 13: 52] unused by ipmi_spd_print()
80           # [ 53] -> [117] module manufacturer JEDEC ID, 0 continuation bytes
2c           # [ 54] -> [118] JEDEC bank 1 code 2Ch = Micron Technology
01           # [ 55] -> [119] module manufacturing location
15           # [ 56] -> [120] manufacturing year 15
23           # [ 57] -> [121] manufacturing week 23
de ad be ef  # [ 58: 61] -> [122:125] serial number deadbeef
@pad 66 0x00   # [ 62:127] never reaches ipmi_spd_print(): the offset 64 read is short
# [128:145] part number, 18 ASCII characters: "18KSF25672AZ-1G4D1"
31 38 4b 53 46 32 35 36 37 32 41 5a 2d 31 47 34 44 31
@pad 110 0x00   # [146:255] unused by ipmi_spd_print()
